WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.497
A common question.

00:00:04.497 --> 00:00:06.995
You have a billion records on disk.

00:00:06.995 --> 00:00:09.492
You want to know if a particular value is in there.

00:00:09.492 --> 00:00:11.990
The naive way: reed the disk until you find it.

00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:14.487
Or reed the whole disk if it isn't.

00:00:14.487 --> 00:00:16.985
There is a much better way.

00:00:16.985 --> 00:00:19.482
It uses kilobytes of RAM and almost no time.

00:00:19.482 --> 00:00:21.980
It is called a Bloom filter.

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:24.477
And it is going to feel like cheating.

00:00:24.477 --> 00:00:27.919
Someone asks, is the song with id forty-two in the Listens table?

00:00:27.919 --> 00:00:31.362
If the answer is no, you skip the disk reed entirely.

00:00:31.362 --> 00:00:34.804
If the answer is yes, you reed that page from disk and search it.

00:00:34.804 --> 00:00:38.246
So the trick is, can we answer "is it in there" fast, without keeping the whole table in memory?

00:00:38.246 --> 00:00:41.689
The short answer: ten-x space savings.

00:00:41.689 --> 00:00:45.131
Constant-time lookup.

00:00:45.131 --> 00:00:48.574
One percent false positives.

00:00:48.574 --> 00:00:52.016
Zero false negatives.

00:00:52.016 --> 00:00:55.490
Here is how it works.

00:00:55.490 --> 00:00:58.965
The Bloom filter is a tiny bit array.

00:00:58.965 --> 00:01:02.439
A few thousand bits.

00:01:02.439 --> 00:01:05.913
All starting at zero.

00:01:05.913 --> 00:01:09.388
For every value in the set, hash it through several functions and turn on the bits at those positions.

00:01:09.388 --> 00:01:12.862
Take a sixteen-bit array.

00:01:12.862 --> 00:01:16.336
INSERT alice hashes alice three times, lands on bits three, seven, and fourteen, sets them.

00:01:16.336 --> 00:01:19.810
Bits nine and eleven were already set by earlier inserts.

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:23.285
To check a new value, hash it the same way and look.

00:01:23.285 --> 00:01:26.759
LOOKUP bob hashes to bits three, nine, and two.

00:01:26.759 --> 00:01:30.233
Bit two is zero.

00:01:30.233 --> 00:01:33.708
So bob is definitely not in the set.

00:01:33.708 --> 00:01:37.182
If any hashed bit is zero, the answer is definitely not in.

00:01:37.182 --> 00:01:40.656
Zero false negatives, ever.

00:01:40.656 --> 00:01:44.131
If every bit is on, the value might be in the set, or other values may have collectively turned those bits on.

00:01:44.131 --> 00:01:47.605
So yes from a Bloom filter means probably.

00:01:47.605 --> 00:01:52.210
Here is why anyone bothers.

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:56.816
Cloudflare puts a Bloom filter in front of its SSD cache, so a file requested exactly once never gets written, sparing billions of useless writes.

00:01:56.816 --> 00:02:01.421
Google and Bing use one to skip pages they have already crawled.

00:02:01.421 --> 00:02:06.026
Sign-up forms use one to tell you a username is taken before any database round trip.

00:02:06.026 --> 00:02:10.632
Same shape every time.

00:02:10.632 --> 00:02:15.237
Ask the filter first, touch the expensive store only on a maybe.

00:02:15.237 --> 00:02:19.225
The probably is tunable.

00:02:19.225 --> 00:02:23.213
Four bits per item gives a ten percent false positive rate.

00:02:23.213 --> 00:02:27.201
Ten bits per item drops to roughly one percent.

00:02:27.201 --> 00:02:31.189
Fourteen bits gets to one in a thousand.

00:02:31.189 --> 00:02:35.177
More bits, fewer collisions, fewer false yeses.

00:02:35.177 --> 00:02:39.165
Pick the point on the curve that fits your workload.

00:02:39.165 --> 00:02:43.153
Most production systems land at ten bits per item.

00:02:43.153 --> 00:02:47.141
For a billion items, that is one and a quarter gigabytes of bits, against eighty gigabytes of RAM for a typical Java hash set on the same data.

00:02:47.141 --> 00:02:49.517
A Bloom filter trades a tiny amount of error for a huge amount of speed.

00:02:49.517 --> 00:02:51.892
The error is bounded.

00:02:51.892 --> 00:02:54.268
Predictable.

00:02:54.268 --> 00:02:56.643
One-sided.

00:02:56.643 --> 00:02:59.019
The speedup is enormous.

00:02:59.019 --> 00:03:01.395
That tradeoff is one of the deepest patterns in systems engineering.

00:03:01.395 --> 00:03:03.770
Bloom filters are where most engineers meet it for the first time.

00:03:03.770 --> 00:03:06.146
Hashing got us one IO per lookup.

00:03:06.146 --> 00:03:08.522
Bloom filters skipped the IO entirely.

00:03:08.522 --> 00:03:10.897
There is one more lever.

00:03:10.897 --> 00:03:13.273
And it works on every query.

00:03:13.273 --> 00:03:15.648
Compression.

00:03:15.648 --> 00:03:18.024
Make the pages smaller.
